                <pb facs="0344" n="30" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg30"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/55</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Whitlock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-02T10:00:00+00:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 2, 1914, 10 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 1:25 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Situation here marked by great uncertainty and apprehension. Difficult secure any
                    information on which to base definite estimate <pb facs="0345" n="31"
                        xml:id="pg31"/> of probable developments. Great financial timidity among
                    classes which has caused runs on all the banks. They are meeting payments and
                    assert that there will be no failures if runs cease within the next few days.
                    Many shops and business houses refuse to accept bank notes although they appear
                    to be well supported. Small coin withdrawn from circulation which causes great
                    difficulty in ordinary transactions. It is hoped that this will be remedied to
                    some extent to-morrow by issue of clearing-house certificates in small
                    denominations. Exchange very high. Banks giving from four francs to four francs
                    fifty for American dollars. Unless I secure unusual information on general
                    situation I shall confine my telegraphic reports to matters affecting Belgium
                    alone.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitlock</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
